 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Victorian women's job market has weakened over the past year, mainly because unemployment has risen. The figures below are seasonally adjusted ABS data for September 2026.\n\n**Latest (September 2026)**\n- **Unemployment rate:** 5.1%. In July 2025 it was 4.2%, and it was about 4.0% in October 2025. It peaked at 5.2% in July 2026.\n- **Participation rate:** 63.6%, down from 63.9% in July 2025. It dipped to 62.4% in April 2026 and has partly recovered.\n- **Employment-to-population ratio:** 60.4%, down from 61.2% in July 2025. It bottomed at 59.5% in April 2026.\n- **Employed women:** about 1.843 million. That is up roughly 13,000 from July 2025, but employment fell to about 1.803 million in April 2026 before recovering.\n- **Full-time employment:** about 1.008 million, down roughly 29,000 from July 2025 (1.038 million). Part-time employment, which I worked out by subtraction, is about 835,000. It has made up the difference, so employment growth has been part-time.\n\n**Compared with men in Victoria**\n- Men's unemployment rate was 5.0% in September 2026, about the same as a year earlier (5.3% in September 2025).\n- Women's unemployment rate has risen from below men's (about 4.0–4.2% in late 2025) to slightly above them (5.1% vs 5.0%).\n- Women's participation is 63.6%, against 71.5% for men.\n\n**Summary**\n- The deterioration was sharpest from March to July 2026.\n- Unemployment has eased slightly from its July peak but is still about a percentage point higher than a year ago.\n- Employment has recovered in headcount, but with fewer full-time jobs.\n- Monthly state-level survey data is volatile, so small month-to-month moves shouldn't be read too closely.",
